	title={Interest groups and the shift from appointed to elected school boards},
	pages={160},
	abstract={The years of 1994 and 1995 saw dramatic changes in Virginia's approach to school governance. During these years many localities elected school board members for the first time. One of these inaugural school board elections was the focus of this study. In particular, this dissertation explored the activity of special interest groups in one Virginia community as it shifted from an appointed to an elected school board. Considering that local conflict between interest groups over educational issues is common, this study endeavored to illustrate the effect of a changing governance structure on the behavior of local interest groups. Three issues surfaced near the time of the first school board election and served both to demonstrate the current activity of interest groups and to identify changes from their previous patterns of interaction. These issues included conflicts over family life education, redistricting of attendance zones, and the school district's annual budget. The primary means of data collection for this study was one-on-one interviews with interest group members, school board members, school board candidates, and local citizens. News paper articles, school board meeting minutes, observations of school board meetings, and campaign materials further supplemented this information. Analysis of interview transcripts identified themes and trends in interest group activity. A narrative, constructed from the data sources listed above, described the story of "Burden" County's inaugural school board election. (Burden is a fictitious name used to maintain the anonymity of the study's participants.) The study's findings show that the change from appointed to elected boards stimulated group formation, involved interest groups in school board election campaigns, and reoriented some interest group lobbying efforts from the local government to the school board. Finally, the shift to elected boards increased some groups sense of efficacy and improved their outlook concerning the responsiveness of local governance. In addition, several group members expressed concern about changing relationships between the school board and the local government.},
	keywords={School administration; Political science},
	url={http://ezproxy.library.wisc.edu/login?url=http://proquest.umi.com/pqdweb?did=739322971&Fmt=7&clientId=12286&RQT=309&VName=PQD}
}

